{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life spanned a turbulent era in the Levant where Christian and Muslim forces clashed constantly. He inherited his title from his father Raymond II who died in an ambush by the Assassins. The young ruler faced immediate challenges because he was a minor at the time of succession. Regency council members managed the county until Raymond reached adulthood. His long reign saw him navigate complex political relationships with other Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nThe death of Raymond II created a power vacuum that threatened the stability of the entire region. Raymond III assumed leadership after a period of regency. He proved himself capable by securing his borders and managing internal disputes among the nobility. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him control over Tiberias and strengthened his influence in Galilee. This strategic union connected him closely to the House of Ibelin and other major families.\n\n*Conflict with the Assassins and Byzantines*\nRaymond III maintained a cautious policy toward the Nizari Ismaili state known as the Assassins. He avoided open war despite previous attacks on his family. His diplomatic approach allowed Tripoli to survive while other states suffered from constant raids. Relations with the Byzantine Empire remained important for trade and military support. He facilitated alliances that brought Greek troops to the aid of Crusader forces during critical moments. His skillful diplomacy kept Tripoli neutral during many conflicts that engulfed neighboring kingdoms.\n\n*Role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Later Years*\nThe death of King Amalric led to succession crises that Raymond III helped resolve. He served as regent for the young Baldwin V and later supported Guy of Lusignan. His opposition to Guy of Lusignan caused significant friction within the royal court. Raymond eventually allied with Saladin against Guy before the disastrous Battle of Hattin. He died shortly after this defeat while fleeing to Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as it faced increasing pressure from Muslim armies. The legacy of his long rule demonstrated the importance of diplomacy over constant warfare. His ability to balance competing interests allowed his realm to endure longer than many expected during such volatile times. The historical record credits him with preserving the integrity of his territory through decades of instability.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0037412804}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768383e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.008057605}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.116516486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.020217285}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.36335617}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.007099618}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.3866888}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.06569485}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.02868791}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.0006903408}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.013614284}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.033073768}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.0063162423}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -4.053124e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -4.768383e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.038762562}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -1.1129332}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.07564395}, {"token": " span", "logprob": -2.4300737}, {"token": "ned", "logprob": -1.4424428e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.028389722}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -0.19286403}, {"token": " era", "logprob": -0.021063432}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.3274338}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.9473078e-05}, {"token": " Lev", "logprob": -0.0957